{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was still a child when his father was assassinated in 1152 and he succeeded him as Count of Tripoli. His mother acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade. *Imprisonment and Release* He was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid and he returned to Tripoli. During his imprisonment his cousin Amalric I of Jerusalem had acted as regent of Tripoli.\n\nUpon his return Raymond III became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. *Regency in Jerusalem* He was appointed regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond III was a member of the king's council and played a key role in the decision making process. He was a strong supporter of the Prince of Antioch and the Count of Edessa and was opposed to the influence of the Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar.\n\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures a widow of Walter of Saint Omer Prince of Galilee. The marriage was childless and Raymond III was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III died in 1187 and was buried in Tripoli. He was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills and played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states. Throughout his life he was a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli and his legacy continued to shape the region for centuries to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.011065074242651463}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264